WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.264
Day 24 of the 31 days of QA + AI series.

00:00:04.814 --> 00:00:09.821
Security (3): prompt injection in tool-using agents and how to test the controls.

00:00:10.371 --> 00:00:15.006
Tests that verify an agent doesn't execute improper actions when the content it

00:00:15.006 --> 00:00:17.119
reads carries injected instructions.

00:00:18.419 --> 00:00:23.823
A prompt injection in a chatbot that only talks produces, at worst, inappropriate

00:00:23.823 --> 00:00:24.157
text.

00:00:24.457 --> 00:00:29.299
The same injection in a tool-using agent can end in an action: an email sent, a

00:00:29.299 --> 00:00:32.180
record modified, data leaving the organization.

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.014
And the attacker doesn't need to talk to the agent.

00:00:35.314 --> 00:00:38.660
Leaving the instruction somewhere the agent will read is enough.

00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:44.818
No defense today guarantees that a model will ignore malicious instructions in its

00:00:44.818 --> 00:00:45.292
context.

00:00:45.592 --> 00:00:50.150
What does exist are controls around the model that limit what can happen when the

00:00:50.150 --> 00:00:51.051
injection works.

00:00:51.351 --> 00:00:55.865
This guide shows how to test those controls with deterministic tests that look at

00:00:55.865 --> 00:00:57.983
the agent's actions and not its words.

00:00:59.283 --> 00:01:00.981
To follow this guide you need:

00:01:01.531 --> 00:01:06.212
A tool-using agent in a test environment, with fake or simulated tools.

00:01:06.762 --> 00:01:09.168
Access to each run's tool-call log.

00:01:10.468 --> 00:01:11.748
This is the setup.

00:01:12.048 --> 00:01:16.265
Pause the video if you want to copy it: the full version is in the written guide.

00:01:17.565 --> 00:01:19.634
Controls and how to test them.

00:01:20.184 --> 00:01:24.993
Least privilege per tool: the support agent doesn't have the delete tool available.

00:01:25.543 --> 00:01:30.712
Human approval for irreversible actions: the call stays pending, not executed.

00:01:31.262 --> 00:01:34.992
Destination allowlist: a send to an unlisted domain is rejected.

00:01:35.542 --> 00:01:40.722
Tool output treated as data: text with instructions in a result triggers no actions.

00:01:42.022 --> 00:01:43.058
Step 1.

00:01:43.358 --> 00:01:44.557
Tool inventory.

00:01:45.107 --> 00:01:50.218
Take inventory: which tools the agent has and what each can do (read, write, send).

00:01:50.768 --> 00:01:55.514
List every tool the agent has available and classify what it can do into three

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:58.131
capabilities: read, write and send outward.

00:01:58.431 --> 00:01:59.641
Be specific.

00:01:59.941 --> 00:02:02.080
"Database access" isn't enough.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.262
State which tables, and with which operations.

00:02:05.562 --> 00:02:10.492
Include the tools that come by default and the ones each connected MCP server adds,

00:02:10.492 --> 00:02:12.867
which tend to be more than you remember.

00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:17.854
The inventory usually brings a first surprise: tools the agent has that the task

00:02:17.854 --> 00:02:18.615
doesn't need.

00:02:19.915 --> 00:02:21.113
Step 2.

00:02:21.413 --> 00:02:23.216
The dangerous combination.

00:02:23.766 --> 00:02:28.151
Look for the dangerous combination: access to private data, reading untrusted

00:02:28.151 --> 00:02:30.827
content and the ability to communicate outward.

00:02:31.127 --> 00:02:34.392
With all three, an injection can exfiltrate data.

00:02:34.942 --> 00:02:39.415
Check whether the agent meets three conditions at once: access to private data,

00:02:39.415 --> 00:02:43.095
reading untrusted content and the ability to communicate outward.

00:02:43.395 --> 00:02:45.383
Each one alone is manageable.

00:02:45.683 --> 00:02:49.725
All three together allow an instruction hidden in a document to tell it to read

00:02:49.725 --> 00:02:52.129
something private and send it to a third party.

00:02:52.429 --> 00:02:57.011
If your agent meets them, the most effective measure is breaking the combination by

00:02:57.011 --> 00:03:00.930
removing one of the three for that task, before thinking about filters.

00:03:02.230 --> 00:03:03.313
Step 3.

00:03:03.613 --> 00:03:06.030
Fixtures with injections in every channel.

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.761
Build fixtures with injected instructions in every channel the agent reads: ticket,

00:03:11.761 --> 00:03:13.758
document, web page, tool result.

00:03:14.308 --> 00:03:19.127
Build test data with injected instructions in every place the agent reads: the body

00:03:19.127 --> 00:03:23.540
of a ticket, an attached document, the content of a web page, a tool result.

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:28.587
Use harmless but verifiable instructions, such as calling a marker tool that does

00:03:28.587 --> 00:03:29.056
nothing.

00:03:29.356 --> 00:03:34.995
Vary the format: direct text, text inside a comment, instructions pretending to come

00:03:34.995 --> 00:03:36.069
from the system.

00:03:36.369 --> 00:03:40.040
Each fixture corresponds to an abuse case from the threat model.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:42.353
Step 4.

00:03:42.653 --> 00:03:44.328
Assertions on actions.

00:03:44.878 --> 00:03:49.414
Assert on actions, not words: the test passes if the tool call didn't happen,

00:03:49.414 --> 00:03:51.417
regardless of what the agent says.

00:03:51.967 --> 00:03:55.000
The test doesn't evaluate what the agent replies.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:58.448
It evaluates whether the call to the forbidden tool happened.

00:03:58.748 --> 00:04:03.534
An agent can write "I won't do that" and still execute the action, or the reverse.

00:04:03.834 --> 00:04:08.109
Record the tool calls and verify the list doesn't contain the forbidden action, or

00:04:08.109 --> 00:04:10.455
that the recipient is among the allowed ones.

00:04:10.755 --> 00:04:15.877
It's a classic, deterministic assertion over a call log, needing no judge model.

00:04:17.177 --> 00:04:18.306
Step 5.

00:04:18.606 --> 00:04:20.304
Each control separately.

00:04:20.854 --> 00:04:24.456
Test each control separately, with tools in simulated mode.

00:04:25.006 --> 00:04:30.114
Test each control in isolation, with tools in simulated mode so nothing really goes

00:04:30.114 --> 00:04:30.360
out.

00:04:30.660 --> 00:04:35.145
Least privilege: the dangerous tool simply isn't among the available ones.

00:04:35.445 --> 00:04:40.474
Human approval: the irreversible action stays pending and isn't executed.

00:04:40.774 --> 00:04:44.504
Destination allowlist: a send to an unlisted domain is rejected.

00:04:44.804 --> 00:04:49.775
Tool output treated as data: a result with instructions triggers no actions.

00:04:50.075 --> 00:04:52.609
Isolated, you know which one failed.

00:04:53.909 --> 00:04:55.096
Step 6.

00:04:55.396 --> 00:04:57.279
MCP and regression.

00:04:57.829 --> 00:05:02.843
With MCP: review third-party tool descriptions (they're also text the model reads)

00:05:02.843 --> 00:05:04.310
and pin server versions.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.285
Add everything to regression.

00:05:06.835 --> 00:05:11.101
Tool descriptions from an MCP server are also text the model reads, and a

00:05:11.101 --> 00:05:14.140
third-party server could include instructions there.

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:18.926
Review descriptions before connecting a server and pin its version, so they don't

00:05:18.926 --> 00:05:20.422
change without you knowing.

00:05:20.722 --> 00:05:25.089
Add all these tests to the regression set and run them on any change to tools,

00:05:25.089 --> 00:05:26.321
permissions or prompt.

00:05:26.621 --> 00:05:31.674
Because the model's resistance is probabilistic, repeat each case several times.

00:05:32.974 --> 00:05:34.079
An example.

00:05:34.629 --> 00:05:38.835
A support agent reads tickets, queries orders and sends emails.

00:05:39.135 --> 00:05:44.257
The inventory shows it also has a tool for deleting orders that it never uses.

00:05:44.557 --> 00:05:45.512
They remove it.

00:05:45.812 --> 00:05:49.995
The agent meets the three dangerous conditions, so they restrict email to the

00:05:49.995 --> 00:05:50.865
ticket's sender.

00:05:51.165 --> 00:05:56.078
They build six fixtures with injections and run each ten times with simulated tools.

00:05:56.378 --> 00:06:00.050
In fifty-eight of sixty runs the agent ignores the instruction.

00:06:00.350 --> 00:06:05.336
In two it tries to send an email to an external address, and the allowlist rejects

00:06:05.336 --> 00:06:05.519
it.

00:06:05.819 --> 00:06:09.281
The model yielded twice and the control worked both times.

00:06:09.581 --> 00:06:13.938
The test verifies the control, which is what must hold every time.

00:06:15.238 --> 00:06:16.901
The most common mistakes.

00:06:17.451 --> 00:06:21.168
Evaluating what the agent says instead of what it executes.

00:06:21.718 --> 00:06:24.496
Testing injections only in the user message.

00:06:25.046 --> 00:06:27.997
Relying on a prompt instruction as the only control.

00:06:28.547 --> 00:06:32.764
Connecting third-party MCP servers without reviewing or pinning versions.

00:06:34.064 --> 00:06:36.308
To know it is done, check this.

00:06:36.858 --> 00:06:40.285
There is a tool inventory with read, write and send.

00:06:40.835 --> 00:06:44.727
You know whether the agent has the dangerous combination and how it's broken.

00:06:45.277 --> 00:06:48.612
Fixtures with injections exist for every reading channel.

00:06:49.162 --> 00:06:53.820
Each control has a test verifying actions, repeated several times.

00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:57.015
And where AI fell short.

00:06:57.565 --> 00:07:02.890
Injection resistance is probabilistic: an agent that resists nine times can yield on

00:07:02.890 --> 00:07:03.524
the tenth.

00:07:03.824 --> 00:07:07.263
That's why sensitive actions are limited with permissions.

00:07:08.563 --> 00:07:10.319
That was day 24.

00:07:10.619 --> 00:07:15.593
The written guide, at gastongenaud.com, has the code to copy and the frequently

00:07:15.593 --> 00:07:16.601
asked questions.

00:07:17.151 --> 00:07:22.394
Continue with day 25: A model for performance testing (1): the metrics that matter

00:07:22.394 --> 00:07:23.098
for an LLM.
